{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Mv3SpJ61zonUfhh1Bb8Ajt","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Prototype: Regex Validator\n2\t\n3\t## Problem\n4\tDevelopers frequently need to test regex patterns against sample text and quickly look up syntax differences across languages. Existing tools are either too minimal (no reference) or too heavy (require accounts, backends). A lightweight, self-contained browser tool with live highlighting and a built-in reference panel would eliminate the context-switching.\n5\t\n6\t## Approach\n7\t- Build as a single `index.html` with inline CSS and JS — zero build step, opens in any browser\n8\t- Three-panel layout via CSS Grid: `[left] [right-top / right-bottom]`\n9\t- Left panel: `<textarea>` for pasting text; on regex match, wrap matched spans in `<mark>` highlights using a content-editable div or overlay technique\n10\t- Right-top panel: `<input>` for the pattern + flag checkboxes (g, i, m, s); run `new RegExp(pattern, flags)` on every keystroke with debounce (~150ms)\n11\t- Right-bottom panel: static HTML reference content in tab groups (one `<section>` per language, toggled via tab buttons)\n12\t- \"Escape for JSON\" button: takes the raw pattern string, runs `JSON.stringify(pattern).slice(1, -1)`, and shows the result in a small output area or copies to clipboard\n13\t- Error state: if `new RegExp()` throws, display the error message in the right-top panel instead of running matching\n14\t\n15\t## Data / State\n16\t- `patternText` — current regex string (string)\n17\t- `flags` — set of active flags (Set<string>)\n18\t- `inputText` — user's pasted content (string)\n19\t- `activeTab` — currently selected reference tab (string, e.g. `\"python\"`)\n20\t- No persistence required for the prototype\n21\t\n22\t## Open Questions\n23\t- Highlight approach: `<textarea>` doesn't support inline styling, so match highlighting requires either a mirrored `<div>` overlay or switching to a `contenteditable` div — need to pick one before building\n24\tResponse: Lets go for `contenteditable` \n25\t- Tab content: how much depth is needed for the reference panel? (Quick-reference cards vs. full syntax tables)\n26\tResponse: Full syntax tables\n27\t- Should the JSON-escape output replace the input or appear separately?\n28\tResponse: Modal window with copy button\n29\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fa55bf36-c1c4-4f7d-8124-96a13dbf10eb","uuid":"cea60939-f925-43a7-8b29-9686eb1ad0f7","timestamp":"2026-04-24T10:26:25.230Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/sysoper/workspace/WhileISleep/batch-output/regex-validator/prototype.md","content":"# Prototype: Regex Validator\n\n## Problem\nDevelopers frequently need to test regex patterns against sample text and quickly look up syntax differences across languages. Existing tools are either too minimal (no reference) or too heavy (require accounts, backends). A lightweight, self-contained browser tool with live highlighting and a built-in reference panel would eliminate the context-switching.\n\n## Approach\n- Build as a single `index.html` with inline CSS and JS — zero build step, opens in any browser\n- Three-panel layout via CSS Grid: `[left] [right-top / right-bottom]`\n- Left panel: `<textarea>` for pasting text; on regex match, wrap matched spans in `<mark>` highlights using a content-editable div or overlay technique\n- Right-top panel: `<input>` for the pattern + flag checkboxes (g, i, m, s); run `new RegExp(pattern, flags)` on every keystroke with debounce (~150ms)\n- Right-bottom panel: static HTML reference content in tab groups (one `<section>` per language, toggled via tab buttons)\n- \"Escape for JSON\" button: takes the raw pattern string, runs `JSON.stringify(pattern).slice(1, -1)`, and shows the result in a small output area or copies to clipboard\n- Error state: if `new RegExp()` throws, display the error message in the right-top panel instead of running matching\n\n## Data / State\n- `patternText` — current regex string (string)\n- `flags` — set of active flags (Set<string>)\n- `inputText` — user's pasted content (string)\n- `activeTab` — currently selected reference tab (string, e.g. `\"python\"`)\n- No persistence required for the prototype\n\n## Open Questions\n- Highlight approach: `<textarea>` doesn't support inline styling, so match highlighting requires either a mirrored `<div>` overlay or switching to a `contenteditable` div — need to pick one before building\nResponse: Lets go for `contenteditable` \n- Tab content: how much depth is needed for the reference panel? (Quick-reference cards vs. full syntax tables)\nResponse: Full syntax tables\n- Should the JSON-escape output replace the input or appear separately?\nResponse: Modal window with copy button\n","numLines":29,"startLine":1,"totalLines":29}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01DLySo3cceKhBe2KCiXhk1m","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a clear picture of what to build. Let me check if there's a MEMORY.md file and the memory directory.\n\nKey requirements:\n1. Single `index.html` with inline CSS and JS — zero build step\n2. Three-panel layout: left panel (text editor with contenteditable div), right-top (regex input + flags), right-bottom (reference panel with tabs)\n3. Live match highlighting using contenteditable approach\n4. \"Escape for JSON\" button showing result in a modal with copy button\n5. Dark mode only\n6. Error state display\n7. Tabs: JS, Python, Go, Java, PCRE\n\n\n8. Full syntax tables for reference tabs\n9. All matches shown simultaneously\n\nI'm structuring this with a CSS Grid layout splitting the viewport into left and right sections, with the right side further divided into pattern input controls on top and a tabbed reference panel below. The left panel uses a contenteditable div to enable inline highlighting of regex matches. When the pattern or text changes, I'll re-render by escaping HTML entities and wrapping matches in mark tags, being careful to preserve cursor position.\n\nBuilding the layout structure... but for a prototype, we can accept some cursor position issues when the regex changes (the text itself doesn't change cursor position, only regex application does)\n\n**State**:\n- `patternText` - current regex string\n- `flags` - Set of active flags\n- `inputText` - user's pasted content\n- `activeTab` - current reference tab\n\n**Debounce**: ~150ms\n\n**JSON escape modal**: `JSON.stringify(pattern).slice(1, -1)` shown in modal with copy button\n\nLet me now write the single `index.html` file. I'll make it comprehensive with full syntax tables for all 5 languages.\n\nFor the contenteditable highlighting approach:\n- Keep `inputText` in sync with the contenteditable's text content\n- On input: update `inputText`, debounce, then re-render highlights\n- Re-render: convert `inputText` to HTML with matches wrapped in `<mark>`\n- Need to preserve cursor position after re-render\n\nFor cursor preservation, I need to save the character offset before re-rendering, update the DOM, then restore the cursor position afterward. Writing helper functions to get and set the caret position...\n\nImplementing the highlight rendering function that escapes HTML and wraps regex matches in mark tags... Writing regex syntax tables... Building the layout structure... Writing cursor position handler... Writing tree walker logic...\n\nNow I'm realizing the real issue is figuring out when to trigger re-renders without doing it unnecessarily.\n\nThe key insight is that we save the cursor position as text character offsets right before updating the HTML, and since we're walking text nodes and ignoring markup, the mark tags won't throw off our restoration logic. I should also handle paste events to strip any rich formatting that might get pasted in.\n\nFor the paste handler, I'm working through the best approach to insert plain text without using deprecated methods. The challenge is managing the selection and text node insertion cleanly while triggering the input event afterward.\n\nNow I'm ready to write out the complete HTML file. I'll organize the regex reference tables into six main categories—character classes, anchors, quantifiers, groups and lookarounds, special characters, and flags—each with pattern, description, and example columns.\n\nI should also handle the \"Escape for JSON\" modal carefully, noting that different languages require different escaping strategies.\n\nWriting the complete HTML... I'm finishing up the color scheme with the tab hover state at `#2e2e2e`, then moving on to implement the full layout. Based on the prototype, I need a two-column design with the left panel for text input and the right side split between regex controls and a reference panel, so I'll structure it with a top bar for the app title, the main content area with those panels, and place the \"Escape for JSON\" button in the regex input section.\n\nBuilding the HTML structure... Writing the reference tables... Writing CSS layout... Still writing styles... Still writing styles... Still writing styles... Still writing styles...\n\nNow I'm planning out the JavaScript logic—I need to set up state management for the input text and regex pattern, grab DOM references for the elements I'll be manipulating, and implement debounced input handlers so the regex matching doesn't fire on every keystroke. The core functionality involves compiling the regex, running it against the input text, and rendering the results with highlighted matches while preserving the cursor position. I'll also need to handle tab switching between different regex reference tables, manage the modal for displaying escaped output, and implement copy-to-clipboard functionality. For the reference content, I'm building comprehensive tables for each language covering character classes, quantifiers, anchors, and other regex features. I'm noting the remaining escape sequences for special characters like null bytes and Unicode code points, then moving into the different flag options available in JavaScript regex. I should also mention that Python's regex module handles named groups and backreferences differently, using a distinct syntax, and has some additional anchors for absolute string boundaries rather than line boundaries. Now I'm looking at how Java handles regex with named groups using angle brackets, backreferences both numeric and named, possessive quantifiers that prevent backtracking, atomic groups for optimization, and Unicode property escapes for matching letter and non-letter characters.\n\nPCRE extends this further with subroutine calls that can reference the entire pattern or specific groups by number or name, conditionals that branch based on whether a group matched, and additional inline flags for case-insensitive matching, multiline mode, dotall mode, and Unicode handling.\n\nNow I'm ready to build out the comprehensive comparison tables and create the full HTML file. For the layout, I'm planning a split view with the left panel taking up half the width, the regex input area on the right fixed at around 120-150 pixels, and the reference section filling the remaining space below. I also need to handle the placeholder text for the contenteditable text editor using CSS.\n\nWriting component styles...\n\nI'm realizing there's a tricky interaction between the contenteditable div and the syntax highlighting. When the user types, the browser updates the DOM immediately, but when I re-render on debounce to apply highlighting, the cursor position gets lost and there's a visual flicker. I need to capture the caret position before re-rendering and restore it afterward, while also handling the case where the user types faster than the debounce interval fires. the highlights. This is fine for UX - users see their text as they type, and highlights appear shortly after.\n\nBut there's a complication: when we set `innerHTML`, the browser might do something unexpected. Let me think...\n\nActually, the better approach might be:\n- Store the raw text separately\n- Only update innerHTML when we want to show highlights\n- When user inputs, update our raw text but don't change innerHTML (let browser handle it)\n- On debounce, set innerHTML to highlighted version\n\nThis is essentially what I described. The key insight is: on debounce fire, we need to:\n1. Save cursor position (as char offset in text content)\n2. Set `el.innerHTML = highlightedHtml`  \n3. Restore cursor position\n\nLet me write this implementation now.\n\nHere's a concern: after setting innerHTML, the cursor will be lost. But we save and restore it, so this should be OK.\n\nThe text editor also needs `spellcheck=\"false\"` and `autocorrect=\"off\"` to prevent the browser from interfering. Now I'm ready to start building the file.\n\nFor the modal, I need to show the JSON-escaped pattern. The modal should display the original pattern and the escaped version (using `JSON.stringify(pattern).slice(1, -1)`), with a copy button for the escaped result and a close button.\n\nI'll also add a note that this escaping is specific to JSON/JavaScript/Python/Java, and other languages might need different handling.\n\nNow I'm writing the complete file systematically: the HTML structure with styles, the reference content for all five languages, the modal markup, and the JavaScript logic for state management, DOM references, and event handlers.\n\nSetting up the JavaScript structure with state variables for input text, pattern text, flags, active tab, and debounce timer.\n\nGrabbing all the DOM elements I'll need...\n\nNow I'm setting up utility functions to handle HTML escaping and manage caret position in the text editor — saving where the cursor is and restoring it after updates. Writing the highlight function... Setting up event handlers for the text editor to trigger regex highlighting with debouncing, and handling paste events to insert plain text while preserving cursor position. Setting up event handlers for the JSON escape modal and copy functionality...\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the `renderHighlights` function and how `innerText` handles whitespace and line breaks—it should work fine since the stored text will preserve the newlines we need. I'm realizing that `escapeHtml` preserves the literal `\\n` character, and since we're using `white-space: pre-wrap`, those newlines should render correctly as actual line breaks in the text node without needing to convert them to `<br>` elements.\n\nThe real issue is that `innerText` can behave inconsistently across browsers and re-renders—sometimes it includes extra whitespace or handles nested elements differently. To keep the text stable and avoid drift, I should walk through the DOM directly, collecting text nodes and converting any `<br>` elements to `\\n` characters, rather than relying on `innerText`'s variable behavior. The real issue is that after we set `innerHTML` with our highlighted content, the next `input` event reads `innerText` which might not match our original `inputText` because the browser has re-parsed the HTML we inserted. So we end up with a mismatch between what we think the user typed and what `innerText` actually returns, which breaks the highlighting on subsequent edits. browser appends to our text node)\n5. `innerText` = \"hello world\" ✓\n\nThis should work fine actually. The text nodes we set in innerHTML are still editable text nodes, and the browser just appends to them.\n\nWhat about when there are `<mark>` tags?\n1. User types \"hello world\", regex = `\\w+`\n2. `innerText` = \"hello world\", `inputText = \"hello world\"`\n3. `applyHighlights()` sets `innerHTML = \"<mark>hello</mark> <mark>world</mark>\"`\n4. User types \"!\" after \"world\" → browser's behavior here is important\n5. The user's cursor was restored after \"world\" (end of mark), so typing \"!\" should add it inside the `<mark>` tag or after it\n\nHmm, this is the tricky part. After highlighting, if the user types, the new character might be inserted inside a `<mark>` tag. Then `innerText` = \"hello world!\" and on the next debounce, the highlights update to match the new text, which is fine. There's a brief visual artifact where \"world!\" sits inside the yellow highlight for ~150ms before the debounce fires, but that's acceptable. The key thing is that `innerText` will reliably return the actual text content regardless of how the DOM gets restructured by the browser's insertion logic.\n\nThe zero-width match edge case is trickier though. If the regex is `^` with the `m` flag on text like \"hello\\nworld\", I get two zero-length matches at the start of each line. My `renderHighlights` function handles this by advancing one character after a zero-length match to avoid infinite loops, but I need to verify this doesn't break the regex's global state. I need to clarify the ES2015 behavior around zero-length matches with global regexes—the engine automatically increments `lastIndex` by 1 after such a match to prevent infinite loops, so I shouldn't manually advance it myself or I'll end up skipping characters. I'm debating whether to highlight zero-length matches like word boundaries—they're technically matches but render as empty marks, which isn't visually useful. I'll skip them for now to keep the prototype simple.\n\nWriting the render function... Writing the reference documentation... Writing the complete HTML file...\n\nI'm thinking through a UX detail for the text editor—when it's empty, I want to show a placeholder message, but `contenteditable` doesn't support that natively. I could use a CSS `::before` pseudo-element with the `:empty` selector, though I need to be careful since setting `innerHTML` might affect whether the div is truly empty in CSS terms. I'll use a data attribute approach with CSS to handle the placeholder — setting `data-empty` in JavaScript and styling it with `attr(data-placeholder)` to keep things clean and avoid edge cases with the `:empty` pseudo-class. Now I'm ready to write out the complete file, organizing the reference tables to stay compact with concise patterns, descriptions, and examples so it doesn't get unwieldy.\n\nWriting the JavaScript sections... Generating reference tables... Writing the HTML file... Still writing styles... Still writing styles... Still writing styles... Still writing styles... Still writing styles... Still writing styles... Still writing modal styles... Still writing styles... Building the regex interface... Building the reference table... Continuing with character classes and ranges, then moving into anchors and boundaries for pattern matching. Generating regex reference table... Finishing up the regex reference table with lazy quantifiers and then moving into groups and capturing patterns. Generating the reference table... Generating the reference table... Building out the Python regex reference with character classes and their behavior across different flag options... Generating markdown table... Generating reference table... Building the regex reference table... Generating regex reference table... Continuing with the regex reference table... Generating regex reference table... Generating the character classes reference table... Generating regex reference table... Building the reference table... Generating reference tables... Building the PCRE reference section... Generating regex reference table...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1101,"cache_read_input_tokens":18271,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1101},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fa55bf36-c1c4-4f7d-8124-96a13dbf10eb","uuid":"fa3951bd-682e-45ac-9597-72aebeb3036d"}
